WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.470
It's 1928 and a Petri dish
left next to an open window

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.560
is about to revolutionise
modern medicine.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.360
Scottish scientist
Alexander Fleming

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.580
finds that something in the
mould growing in the dish

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.580
is killing off bacteria.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.920
It's penicillin, the
first antibiotic.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.940
This wonder drug, and
other antibiotics,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.710
will go on to treat
the untreatable,

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.030
saving millions of lives.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.690
It's 2050.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.890
The cupboard is bare.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.173
Antibiotics were our
precious resource.

00:00:47.173 --> 00:00:48.090
We needed to use them.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.140
Of course we did.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.230
But we weren't prudent.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.510
Because each time we
used them, misused them,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.750
and overused them, we
gave the bacteria a chance

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.180
to evolve, to become resistant.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.940
And without global
leadership scientists

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.480
have been unable to bring
new antibiotics to market.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.790
Now, even cuts have
fatal consequences.

00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:15.340
Has the clock
really been ticking

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.610
towards a
post-antibiotic future,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.960
where drug-resistant infections
kill 10m people a year?

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.990
Where chemotherapy is unsafe?

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.310
Where simple surgeries
are too risky to perform?

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.450
Where the world's biggest
child-killer, pneumonia,

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.630
is now unstoppable?

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:33.270
Is this just a projection?

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.090
2020.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.250
Climate change is headline news.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:44.630
Forests burn.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.372
Scientists predict
rising sea levels.

00:01:47.372 --> 00:01:49.580
There is a sense that time
for action is running out.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.530
And then comes Covid.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:59.613
It's a fast-moving pandemic.

00:01:59.613 --> 00:02:00.780
A million dead and counting.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:08.080
But the world
mobilises with urgency.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.560
Individuals, doctors,
scientists, yes, governments

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.380
and policymakers, too.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.760
The unthinkable
becomes the norm.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.870
Cities, countries
under lockdown.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.660
Economies radically reshaped.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.570
Vaccines developed
in record time.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.523
And against this
backdrop, a chance

00:02:31.523 --> 00:02:33.690
to change the narrative on
that other looming global

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.700
catastrophe, the
one we already know

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.340
about - antibiotic resistance.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.890
Because everyone sees
now, we can mobilise.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.610
We can take individual and
collective responsibility,

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:44.610
and we must be prepared.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.150
It's 1945.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.250
The Allied victory.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.240
That was a collective
effort, too.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.030
During World War Two penicillin
has been mass produced,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.860
used to treat soldiers'
septic wounds.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.730
Hailed as a victory
of science over death.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:05.540
But as he collects
his Nobel Prize,

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.870
Fleming is already
issuing a warning.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.150
Misusing antibiotics
can hasten the evolution

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.550
of drug-resistant
bacteria and render them

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.260
useless for future generations.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.970
Here's how it works with E
coli in a giant Petri dish.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:29.670
The E coli bacteria hits a
line of antibiotic and pause,

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.770
until a random mutation
enables the bacteria

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.510
to overcome the drug.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.050
At each line, the antibiotic
is 10 times more concentrated,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.810
but there are also
more mutations.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.610
It's evolution by
natural selection,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.840
only very, very fast.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.300
We live in a Petri dish, too.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.975
The bacteria are a
part of our ecosystem

00:03:54.975 --> 00:03:56.970
- around us, within us.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.820
Trillions upon trillions,
helpful as well as harmful.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.290
And resistance is
natural, to be expected.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:12.600
But by increased misuse we
only encouraged the mutations.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:13.650
We overprescribed.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:14.790
Of course we did.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.740
Medicine is not
an exact science.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:21.988
Diagnosis is difficult, and
people expect to be cured.

00:04:21.988 --> 00:04:24.030
In some parts of the world
antibiotics don't even

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.155
need a prescription at all.

00:04:25.155 --> 00:04:28.130
They're over the counter or
at the back of the drawer.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.750
But against viral coughs,
colds, and sore throats,

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.820
they're useless -
worse than useless.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.900
We give them to our
farm animals, too.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.460
We want to prevent infection.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.810
We want to plump our food.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.830
We want cheap meat.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.100
It's the 1980s.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.090
This is the start of
the discovery void.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.960
For more than 30 years no
new classes of antibiotics

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.040
will be launched.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.060
Why?

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.140
Follow the money,
and it runs out.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.570
Big Pharma has no incentive
to invest in the development

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:09.710
of antibiotics.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:10.910
There's no profit.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:15.440
It's expensive, time consuming,
and complex, and single use.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.480
While antidepressants
and heart drugs - they

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.145
make money on repeat
Meanwhile, controls

00:05:20.145 --> 00:05:21.770
designed to limit
the inappropriate use

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.103
of antibiotics also limit
financial incentives

00:05:24.103 --> 00:05:24.770
for making them.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:35.450
It's 2020, the here and now,
and we have some of the answers.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:38.570
Preventing infections with
better hygiene and vaccines.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:40.460
We can repackage
existing antibiotics

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:41.750
to make them resistance-proof.

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:45.560
Or use phage therapy to attack
the bacteria with viruses.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.740
And in the future AI could lead
to more accurate diagnoses.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.300
Surely new antibiotics must
be part of the answer, too.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.300
Funding agreements can
give the bigger companies

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.160
the financial incentive.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.500
Partnerships can give smaller
companies access to resources.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.860
And what about de-linking
volume from profit?

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.830
Concentrating not on the number
of pills but their value.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.620
Paying companies not for how
much antibiotic they provide,

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:11.720
but its potential worth
to the health system.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.310
Something like a subscription.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:14.600
Something like insurance.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.480
The building is catching fire.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.370
The edifice is crumbling.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.910
The cupboard is running bare.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:40.030
Already, antibiotic resistance
is killing 700,000 people

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.020
a year.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.630
In some ways Covid has
made the problem worse.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.310
Fears about secondary
infections mean

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:47.140
large quantities
of antibiotics have

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.360
been prescribed to patients.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.670
But Covid could help
make things better, too.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:54.340
If it motivates us, if
the world mobilises -

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.940
individuals, doctors,
scientists, yes, governments

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:59.560
and policy makers, too.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.410
The solutions are scientific,
but they're also economic,

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.310
and they're societal.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.380
It's a collective effort.

00:07:08.823 --> 00:07:09.740
It's not a projection.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.310
It's not a sort of an
imaginary projection

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.518
that it's going to get worse.

00:07:12.518 --> 00:07:13.873
We know that's going to happen.

00:07:13.873 --> 00:07:15.790
And because we know that
it's going to happen,

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.370
we actually also know
how we can slow it down.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.828
You realise that actually all
those things that you prepare

00:07:20.828 --> 00:07:23.310
for - it's so that you've got
it there when you need it.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.080
Everyone has their part to play.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.210
The point is we do
have the opportunity

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.690
to do something about it.

